# do daily/weekly/monthly maintenance
# min	hour	day	month	weekday	command
*/15	*	*	*	*	run-parts /etc/periodic/15min
0	*	*	*	*	run-parts /etc/periodic/hourly
0	2	*	*	*	run-parts /etc/periodic/daily
0	3	*	*	6	run-parts /etc/periodic/weekly
0	5	1	*	*	run-parts /etc/periodic/monthly

# Low-downtime VACUUM FULL via dump/restore/promote (weekly_vacuum_
# swap.py's own docstring has the full mechanism). Changed 2026-09-04
# from every Monday to the FIRST Monday of each month: it exists purely
# to reclaim disk space in the LIVE DB's on-disk footprint (regular
# autovacuum already keeps the table usable/performant on its own, it
# just doesn't shrink the file back down) -- separate from backup size,
# which was never affected by live bloat either way (pg_dump/
# postgres_bak.sh only ever dumps live row data, not dead tuples/free
# space, regardless of bloat). Not worth picasa_api downtime every
# single week for that; monthly is the compromise.
#
# REAL BUG, found and fixed 2026-10-19: the original entry was
# "0 3 1-7 * 1" (day-of-month 1-7, weekday Monday). Cron's documented
# semantics: when BOTH day-of-month and day-of-week are restricted
# (neither is "*"), the job fires if EITHER matches -- not both. So
# this was actually firing every Monday (matching that previously
# every-Monday entry anyway) AND every day 1-7 of the month, up to
# several times a month instead of once -- confirmed via
# picasa_prevacuum_2026_10_07 (a Wednesday) and _10_12 (a Monday)
# both already sitting on disk from recent weeks. Fixed by scheduling
# every Monday here (no day-of-month restriction, so no AND/OR
# ambiguity) and moving the real "only the FIRST Monday" check into
# the script itself (plain AND logic in Python, unambiguous) -- see
# weekly_vacuum_swap.py's own day-of-month guard. Still Monday 3am
# specifically so it doesn't collide with the daily backup (2am) or
# the stock weekly slot above (Saturday, weekday 6).
0	3	*	*	1	python3 /etc/periodic/weekly_vacuum_swap.py --promote --only-first-monday >> /var/log/weekly_vacuum_swap.log 2>&1
